= Weed Control

Commerce City's dry, sunny climate, alkaline soil, and clay texture create a
specific weed pressure profile. This section covers weed control across your
three main landscape zones: lawn, mulched planting beds, and crushed rock areas.

== Lawn Weed Control

*Pre-emergent in spring:* Apply a pre-emergent herbicide
(#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=prodiamine+pre+emergent+lawn")[prodiamine] or
#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=pendimethalin+lawn+weed+control")[pendimethalin])
in mid-April when forsythia blooms or soil temperatures reach 50–55°F consistently
at 4-inch depth. This prevents crabgrass and most annual grass weeds. Do NOT apply
if you plan to overseed that fall — wait at least 8–10 weeks after overseeding, or
8–12 weeks before seeding after pre-emergents.

*Post-emergent broadleaf weeds:* For dandelions, clover, bindweed, and other
broadleaf weeds, apply a selective broadleaf herbicide containing
#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=three+way+broadleaf+herbicide+lawn+Colorado")[2,4-D, dicamba, and MCPP]
(Ortho Weed-B-Gon, Spectracide Weed Stop, or Bayer BioAdvanced) in May or September
when weeds are actively growing and temperatures are below 85°F. Do not mow for
2 days before or after. Do not apply when wind is above 10 mph.

*Crabgrass:* If pre-emergent was missed, spot-treat with post-emergent quinclorac
(Drive XLR8 or similar) when plants are young (2–4 tillers). Large established
patches are best managed by improving lawn density through aeration and overseeding.

*Mowing as weed control:* The 2.5–3 inch CSU-recommended mowing height is your
best passive weed defense. Dense tall turf shades out weed seedlings more
effectively than short-cut lawn.

*Bindweed in lawn edges:*
#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=bindweed+control+lawn+Colorado")[Field bindweed]
(_Convolvulus arvensis_) spreads by both seed and deep rhizome. Repeated
applications of broadleaf herbicide containing triclopyr + 2,4-D in late summer
(August–September) is the most effective control. Persistence over 2–3 seasons
is required.

== Mulched Planting Bed Weed Control

*Pre-emergent in beds:* Apply a granular pre-emergent labeled for landscape beds
(#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=snapshot+herbicide+landscape+beds")[Snapshot],
#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=preen+garden+weed+preventer")[Preen], or
#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=dimension+herbicide+landscape")[Dimension])
in late March or early April before weed seeds germinate. Reapply in early July
for season-long control.

*Mulch depth:* 2–3 inches of organic mulch (shredded wood, cedar, pine bark) is
the most effective physical weed barrier. Weed seeds germinating in mulch have
shallow, weak roots that are easy to hand-pull. Maintain mulch depth annually.
Avoid piling mulch against plant crowns.

*Annual and perennial weeds:* For annual weeds: hand-pull or hoe while young,
before they set seed. A
#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=stirrup+hoe+weeding+tool")[stirrup hoe]
makes quick work of seedlings. For perennial weeds (bindweed, dandelion, thistle):
spot-treat with glyphosate applied carefully to weed foliage only, or use a
foam-tip applicator to protect adjacent plants.

*Bindweed in beds:* Pull vines weekly for an entire season — preventing the leaves
from photosynthesizing progressively starves the root. Spot-treat with glyphosate
or triclopyr on a windless day. Control is a multi-year effort.

*Grass weeds in beds:*
#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=fluazifop+grass+herbicide+landscape+beds")[Fluazifop]
(Ornamec, Grass-B-Gon) selectively kills grassy weeds without harming broadleaf
perennials and shrubs — effective for lawn grass encroaching into bed edges.

== Crushed Rock Area Weed Control

*Landscape fabric:* If not already installed, woven landscape fabric (not plastic
sheeting) beneath crushed rock is the primary barrier. Note that windblown organic
material accumulates on top of rock over time and supports weed germination regardless
of fabric — this surface layer must be managed.

*Pre-emergent in rock:* Apply the same products (Preen, Snapshot) to crushed rock
areas in spring. Broadcast on the rock surface and water in.

*Post-emergent in rock:* Non-selective herbicides including
#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=glyphosate+herbicide+use")[glyphosate]
(Roundup) and commercial-strength horticultural vinegar (20–30% acetic acid) are
effective for active weeds in rock areas. Always avoid contact with desirable plants.

*Organic debris:* Rake out accumulated leaves, soil, and organic debris from rock
areas annually in spring — this organic layer is where most weed seeds germinate.
A thorough spring cleanup reduces weed pressure through the entire season.
